<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bao Shuya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bao Shuya was a minister of the State of Qi in the seventh century BCE and the lifelong friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-qi">Duke Huan of Qi</a> won the throne, Bao persuaded him to appoint Guan Zhong, who had served Huan's rival and even shot at him, as chief minister, which led to Qi's rise as the leading state. Their friendship became proverbial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bao Shuya was a native of Yingshang, in present-day Anhui Province, as was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a>. His family was relatively wealthy compared with Guan's, but the two became close friends in youth, and Bao admired his friend's talent. Before entering politics they often took part in business ventures together. Guan Zhong later recalled that he took more of the profits for himself and that Bao did not think him greedy but understood his poverty, and that when his ideas lost money Bao blamed the fluctuations of business rather than his judgement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The two friends entered politics as tutors to two princes of Qi: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a> to Prince Jiu, and Bao Shuya to Prince Xiaobai, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-qi">Duke Huan of Qi</a>. Under Duke Xiang of Qi, a dissolute ruler, Bao foresaw disorder and fled with Xiaobai to the neighbouring State of Ju, while Guan went with Jiu to the State of Lu. When Duke Xiang was killed by one of his ministers, who was murdered in turn, the princes became rivals for the throne. Bao brought Xiaobai back to Qi first, and the new duke pressed Lu into killing Prince Jiu and sending Guan Zhong back.</p><p>Guan had shot an arrow at Xiaobai that struck his belt buckle, and the duke wished to execute him while making Bao, his tutor and guardian, chancellor. Bao persuaded him first to spare Guan and then to raise him to the chancellorship above Bao himself, and he convinced Guan to serve the new duke wholeheartedly. As an official Bao was known as a judge of character and talent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 645 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a> fell seriously ill, Duke Huan asked him about a successor and proposed Bao. Guan advised against it: Bao was too honest, upright and unyielding, and since he expected others to meet the exacting standards he set himself, he would find it hard to work with the rest of the court. Bao outlived his friend by a year.</p><p>Chinese tradition remembers him for his loyalty, tolerance and judgement of ability, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a>'s career and the later power of Qi can arguably be traced to Bao's faith in his friend. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> records Guan's words: &quot;My parents gave birth to me, but it is Bao who knows me best.&quot; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> himself remarked that men of Guan Zhong's talent are not rare, but men like Bao Shuya who can recognise talent are.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bao Shuya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yingshang County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.64,116.26&amp;year=-635" aria-label="Yingshang County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bao Shuya"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4731 4770h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4731 4770h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.64,116.26&amp;year=-635">What was here in 635 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
